${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "352a7d2c-4aba-5246-8a20-ed79de007b95" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Matrix Expression Involving the Hadamard Product is Well-Defined" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048122 :topic/id #uuid "80c022c4-6a8a-5129-a159-ed3a1eacb632" :topic/math-academy-id 5916 :topic/title "The Hadamard Product"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1a4696d-b69f-5d96-8a99-a5ac54f14d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473022} {:answer/id #uuid "e6122a9e-0055-56ab-9e70-260870089c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1a4696d-b69f-5d96-8a99-a5ac54f14d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473022} :answer-field/id #uuid "32df3386-8717-5771-8026-5b9b3d6f3a97" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea01be3f-c463-5c26-afd1-79fdd3160fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473024} {:answer/id #uuid "35039e78-90c6-5078-8bd2-c933eddfdc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea01be3f-c463-5c26-afd1-79fdd3160fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473024} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e66d9b-3e91-59b2-87c1-33b85b052abb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ddf762a-6c2f-5b24-8b46-530b5f91ca6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473026} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9c62bf-3b83-5cec-82e2-33d8ec17c7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9c62bf-3b83-5cec-82e2-33d8ec17c7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473027} :answer-field/id #uuid "041adbf9-7b59-5e9f-87a1-786e7bb800ac" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6deba07-cdc0-5243-9183-e0064a49692b" :question/math-academy-id "q-274938" :question/problem "Given the matrices\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n in which of the following expressions is the Hadamard product well-defined?\n- **I.** $YX⊙Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$\n- **II.** $X⊙Yu$\n- **III.** $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)⊙YX{X}^{T}$\n\n*Hint: Here, $\\text{diag}(v)$ is a diagonal matrix with the entries of $v$ on the diagonal.*\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nFirst, notice that $X$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $u$ is a $3\\times 1$ column vector.\n\nNow, let's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of $YX$ are $2\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $X$ are $3\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $Yu$ are $2\\times 1.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$ are $2\\times 3,$ and the dimensions of $YX{X}^{T}$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048122 :topic/id #uuid "80c022c4-6a8a-5129-a159-ed3a1eacb632" :topic/math-academy-id 5916 :topic/title "The Hadamard Product"} :activity/title "The Hadamard Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416950} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416983}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7384a9a6-a6f3-509e-8604-32e6ff1cf91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453416} {:answer/id #uuid "d926c783-6a31-5aad-8676-68e3ed986684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d926c783-6a31-5aad-8676-68e3ed986684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453417} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16f14e7-1180-5cad-8b96-837d1cc5647f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454458} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f86d39fb-1e90-5901-85fd-3a8ced53e62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453418} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e62c65-eae5-5cc0-b334-901e889e9707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e62c65-eae5-5cc0-b334-901e889e9707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453419} :answer-field/id #uuid "3acee59e-520e-5e8f-8529-6999b135978a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31cc429d-07c8-5275-a610-638d58689d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453420} {:answer/id #uuid "8469e1a3-49e3-5d1c-a349-3f76f08bd629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8469e1a3-49e3-5d1c-a349-3f76f08bd629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453421} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a7e42a-5e2f-5d59-ab7e-86b6db1851a4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454460} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332b4d60-25b6-5bcb-b419-6cb43732aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453422} {:answer/id #uuid "e402ecbd-276a-5177-b646-7e13763d957b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "332b4d60-25b6-5bcb-b419-6cb43732aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453422} :answer-field/id #uuid "eab3e9d9-7d15-5b97-a15c-d97464e94366" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feee3179-e7d1-55ab-bcac-f334cca10495" :question/math-academy-id "q-156620" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $4x=2y$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $4x=2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =2y \\\\ 4(0) & =2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space"} :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417212} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417223}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048920 :topic/id #uuid "cc887b1d-8841-507d-a99b-b7888c08a675" :topic/math-academy-id 1728 :topic/title "Finding the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Using Row Operations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe7e325-2fed-5fe6-82f1-fde3a0369519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473320} {:answer/id #uuid "2707c8dd-4e18-5590-9551-822813770a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe7e325-2fed-5fe6-82f1-fde3a0369519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473320} :answer-field/id #uuid "68674cd9-a7ae-5bc2-a4d9-c026495c3629" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e4d87a-9878-5194-abc8-b8e409575c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473322} {:answer/id #uuid "8b25371e-e8f0-570c-abbe-a8239bd51334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b25371e-e8f0-570c-abbe-a8239bd51334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473323} :answer-field/id #uuid "38075992-4d8f-549c-bf5e-547a44778fde" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63df3d5f-9a6d-5ff0-bf8a-a6cdee7d5643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473324} {:answer/id #uuid "87e93244-9d30-5d8e-bfc9-3dc22fafd49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63df3d5f-9a6d-5ff0-bf8a-a6cdee7d5643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473324} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed8b7f4-58be-52a4-9b60-74f11f144b74" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b47d0cc7-71aa-58ba-a792-0650346f33fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75505" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050493 :topic/id #uuid "4db2b54f-79ed-566a-a082-ccbcb95e93a8" :topic/math-academy-id 4821 :topic/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4641dd2b-0c33-5e1b-8fb6-e1732f1ca632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472964} {:answer/id #uuid "df9d34f1-9e8a-5c1d-9127-7a693044637c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df9d34f1-9e8a-5c1d-9127-7a693044637c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472965} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd9ded88-5b01-5f18-8c7f-8184b4b086a7" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d43e7b-bb09-52fe-9995-24a5a4a57ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472966} {:answer/id #uuid "4e79306d-6776-5c67-a529-9325cea238fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e79306d-6776-5c67-a529-9325cea238fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472967} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b2d8c1c-a517-52af-9082-bd7bd2dbbf45" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da95c20f-dc58-5edc-8dcb-dd5058ac04ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472968} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7455f8-3833-5279-8d9e-7da8601cff21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab7455f8-3833-5279-8d9e-7da8601cff21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472969} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58adf13-8680-5744-bc7c-9ac757d84107" :answer-field/key "T" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16c25cec-ba90-5814-9c8d-34f1bb30c2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213599" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **R.** $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- **S.** $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y=0,$ defined on $R$\n\n- **T.** $X\\,T\\,Y$ if and only if $|X|=|Y|,$ defined on $P(N)$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ then $(y,x)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in S$ then $(y,x)\\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⟹\\,y+x=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y)\\in T$ then $(Y,X)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n|X|=|Y|\\,⟹\\,|Y|=|X|\n$$\n\nfor all $X,Y\\in P(N).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$, $S,$ and $T.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050493 :topic/id #uuid "4db2b54f-79ed-566a-a082-ccbcb95e93a8" :topic/math-academy-id 4821 :topic/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e40c48dc-e112-5050-ab58-40e28aca37d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472970} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc8cd32-07de-52bc-9812-9df201cd21ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e40c48dc-e112-5050-ab58-40e28aca37d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472970} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3966c5d-4fd4-576a-aa5a-8af4208f68ce" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db41ede4-d39a-594f-9cbf-c3280e5f8333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472972} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef80bb4-d9c1-5bcb-affd-78fc449eb48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db41ede4-d39a-594f-9cbf-c3280e5f8333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472972} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b39b89-1336-54cf-abaa-62896c6eb4d0" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473881} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70a073a2-a27e-5ccd-9655-045b4e8967e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472974} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8fe957-451b-59db-8c93-9d1560fedb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f8fe957-451b-59db-8c93-9d1560fedb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472975} :answer-field/id #uuid "0010c237-26c5-5528-a141-70b3e174715b" :answer-field/key "T" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe8a5755-a006-512f-a829-ac55db45a0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-213600" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **R.** $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $y={x}^{2},$ defined on $R$\n\n- **S.** $X\\,S\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊂Y,$ defined on $P(R)$\n\n- **T.** $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $4∣(x+y),$ defined on $N$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(-1,1)\\in R$ since $1=(-1)^{2},$ while\n\n- $(1,-1)\\notin R$ since $-1\\ne {1}^{2}.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $({1},Z)\\in S$ since ${1}⊂Z,$ while\n\n- $(Z,{1})\\notin S$ since $Z⊄{1}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ then $(y,x)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣(x+y)\\,⟹\\,4∣(y+x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a8fd7b-d587-5f84-abef-00aeb093eb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453892} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c74f36-a3a0-589c-a45b-af2f83dc7942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5a8fd7b-d587-5f84-abef-00aeb093eb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453892} :answer-field/id #uuid "88425be4-23e4-530d-894d-7ba2ee67cacc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b1c9af-14b9-5566-a390-e0cfcfb93713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453894} {:answer/id #uuid "56beb87c-fb76-5782-843d-1bb2ef34ea37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56beb87c-fb76-5782-843d-1bb2ef34ea37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453895} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eccd02e-cd48-5da5-a4d4-40a643e437ff" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454697} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62cc4c4f-6b01-5029-937b-d1096d770d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453896} {:answer/id #uuid "998a225c-9aad-55b1-9522-e1b176e1886c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62cc4c4f-6b01-5029-937b-d1096d770d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453896} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b61e149-64e1-57db-98c8-d0a11bc647eb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0464e7a7-ce22-55cd-90ec-4b6a71a12079" :question/math-academy-id "q-38893" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417684} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417707}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dae18-53b8-50d8-9953-c286f053f130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473452} {:answer/id #uuid "f544fe4b-2071-53b8-8fd7-eb03478e4a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07dae18-53b8-50d8-9953-c286f053f130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473452} :answer-field/id #uuid "2548af25-17a2-5afb-af86-44ee79ccc156" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474121} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fea2177-f886-5ea3-bcfa-edb19fb1d593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473454} {:answer/id #uuid "210dadc5-e8c8-5ceb-a5ce-5ab0c4ff06d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "210dadc5-e8c8-5ceb-a5ce-5ab0c4ff06d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473455} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e057aa0-f318-5406-860f-b5b16d8d2d93" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff6fa49d-2a3b-50f4-b709-7cf191c1f26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473456} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1113bc-9c71-5f2b-98a5-ba108659a934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff6fa49d-2a3b-50f4-b709-7cf191c1f26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473456} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f0c4b97-3dd5-51e1-96d0-6bc6a154c613" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c77aeb41-578d-533f-87d1-e33216f35c1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-84053" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0$ and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$\n- **III.** $T$ is invertible\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is a linear transformation and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ we have $T({u}_{1}-{u}_{2})=0.$ So ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3,$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417684} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417705}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f955c119-f900-5747-9855-7eb87295f2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473260} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc29abc-f9e8-53f8-a218-d470e670dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc29abc-f9e8-53f8-a218-d470e670dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473261} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b57ac4-5833-5f98-85f6-8d01d8cfbdee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da87805c-dd59-5cd4-a00e-ea9010aca456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473262} {:answer/id #uuid "ce41ce68-62e4-5bd8-8ba7-9e969d0aef89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da87805c-dd59-5cd4-a00e-ea9010aca456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473262} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f96f24-dff3-5ad2-ae8b-117ac4e4cad2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474026} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eb19004-1309-5d14-8763-0cdb11e1bbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473264} {:answer/id #uuid "64416903-13ab-5932-b3c4-6697b70d4774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eb19004-1309-5d14-8763-0cdb11e1bbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473264} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b6a004-2b46-516a-adc3-ac69774aa23d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4db015a0-682b-5ff4-909b-ad19f05ab168" :question/math-academy-id "q-71826" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b838b945d0b4002d8ae2238af5992c83dcf23b2985c448c716a5caf8de31b369.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/be/be1ece00243777f3f6b6973319e15ea1e5c8556b2452b61a451713f9dcb86595.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"} :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417784} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417819}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb12b54-0323-5408-9f63-bd359e7b2e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453448} {:answer/id #uuid "164d2254-207a-594f-a70d-382d51b3f8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "164d2254-207a-594f-a70d-382d51b3f8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453449} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5032cdf-c59e-5e33-880d-955f6d02841e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3427dac4-97f1-5765-aafc-94068cf9677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453450} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0ad7a7-e7bc-5678-aca2-5d4474e68307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3427dac4-97f1-5765-aafc-94068cf9677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3387072-c1bc-59ee-8d45-dc6cd0e5411d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ad1ee2-a34e-5097-bfd1-c65783c31fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453452} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0e000c-633e-51cc-bcaf-35de257a2bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ad1ee2-a34e-5097-bfd1-c65783c31fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453452} :answer-field/id #uuid "feaa10ce-c2f4-5d9a-998c-4aa5896d0d74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e4c0564-b5b1-55bc-bc17-3dffa3a951a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158601" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(1,-1)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/75/75d5d199d12cc2f3170822a40c52762fcc8368caac26a8134661f9ac72ab462b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/818da293be040897d3a536b396d6fe7ff992f3d91f976301e14a6253356b5bde.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=-1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<-1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"} :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417784} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417823}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca900fdb-2d62-5862-8f58-5e2c93efbb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453436} {:answer/id #uuid "839c8d82-74a4-5464-b707-309afd311002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "839c8d82-74a4-5464-b707-309afd311002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453437} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5b6f56-ac9e-5312-8788-c82ee0344df5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb2be68-e58e-5cf5-a802-398a76d09aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453438} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f7b94-44bb-5eab-8fad-f6e5da807f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f7b94-44bb-5eab-8fad-f6e5da807f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453439} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d1f656-b70c-5cbc-b720-c740ebcddde4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454469} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66605df2-8aaf-504d-949d-2dc2c5eb81cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453440} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb9751c-c1ad-5871-aa80-62619467576e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbb9751c-c1ad-5871-aa80-62619467576e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453441} :answer-field/id #uuid "54080e51-b892-57e7-b433-ee3c85f02bbe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbe53a96-4c4c-5897-ba48-d4861ec6cb49" :question/math-academy-id "q-158547" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>2x-3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $Q(0,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x-3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/5a/5af40a400e5ea4942a1cf89b09184a277df96d252e4a7fee41bd2df7024a631f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27b978864eac65c93c7886b2d54dfede7130f0f93a16ef96421cd0e5741a6940.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=2x-3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the coordinates of $Q(0,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y>2x-3.$ Hence, $Q$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"} :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417784} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417822}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c50983-8391-5870-8348-d7f660e46076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472748} {:answer/id #uuid "60e11f58-a5dd-5c6e-b89c-b922fde1c5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60e11f58-a5dd-5c6e-b89c-b922fde1c5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472749} :answer-field/id #uuid "143a9e68-361d-54b4-9f3d-e48cc583f56a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6913eb54-9ddc-554e-82da-aa99b31eea06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472750} {:answer/id #uuid "03c27322-4cea-5897-a805-a6c4a07342da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03c27322-4cea-5897-a805-a6c4a07342da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472751} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c72ab19-8f7b-5385-a77a-7bded7712b10" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f94601-7133-564b-ac38-53504ef01422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472752} {:answer/id #uuid "b58541fc-27f2-5130-a4c2-74d24b8dffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b58541fc-27f2-5130-a4c2-74d24b8dffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9085bd-5d71-5a86-abfb-326a5e169143" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7105850-e717-5535-8a28-db3aaf9f3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-159495" :question/problem "![](images/19/19174ec33e43c900773031043d5de200e892f5e3ee51704d0800b6a944147155.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/43/43362800e91cbb57c22fc6851c10f00927a030a790dea9284e69a5c7c3d4ae25.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"} :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417784} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417820}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050726 :topic/id #uuid "61838998-9541-5ba0-829a-1421ece4cbfb" :topic/math-academy-id 3132 :topic/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bf1c7c-6b0e-563e-954c-3195f51957e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454208} {:answer/id #uuid "da6a0750-c545-55b0-96a6-f69075551811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44bf1c7c-6b0e-563e-954c-3195f51957e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454208} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5818556-f363-51a8-99fb-0d3f96f4adeb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8908ac85-0ea3-5a1c-b891-38df1e4efa1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454210} {:answer/id #uuid "c681945d-6fe4-577f-97ef-2ee6b13ef95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c681945d-6fe4-577f-97ef-2ee6b13ef95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454211} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f45678-b73d-5101-b847-456979724eed" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a3a2869-1fca-522a-9ef5-456efad708a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454212} {:answer/id #uuid "0d94671b-f829-5477-b5c4-2a0aa1a5da65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a3a2869-1fca-522a-9ef5-456efad708a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454212} :answer-field/id #uuid "0749b39f-5020-520c-b5a7-35e6e06dd907" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d3ee109-94d4-53ed-8971-ac2ef97dce27" :question/math-academy-id "q-95901" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Σ$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- **II.** $V$ is an orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- **III.** $U$ is a diagonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $3\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix,the matrix $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $U$ must be orthogonal but not necessarily diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050335 :topic/id #uuid "40d51ec6-50e2-56a3-b67d-694483590810" :topic/math-academy-id 4822 :topic/title "Transitive Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f66032c-e554-543d-8fb7-d02dece4fb60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472988} {:answer/id #uuid "503722a1-9d9c-52aa-81fc-6a411eae1066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "503722a1-9d9c-52aa-81fc-6a411eae1066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472989} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded48b57-0e55-5867-8ce5-09520070f5ca" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4b3082-da87-5935-8a6d-c02d7c283455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472990} {:answer/id #uuid "60bece86-b150-57aa-9c00-90ecd30c70e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60bece86-b150-57aa-9c00-90ecd30c70e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472991} :answer-field/id #uuid "755bfb35-b9d2-5062-b4ab-e488a159dfa7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "279c4aba-6d55-5a04-b286-e063ea4fc0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472992} {:answer/id #uuid "733b09d3-0ca0-56fd-b468-a6f12122fc9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "733b09d3-0ca0-56fd-b468-a6f12122fc9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472993} :answer-field/id #uuid "242b2c29-1e00-59da-9422-d72051cd8ab7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473891} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbf5caaf-107c-5cf9-9a1b-17712be4c02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472994} {:answer/id #uuid "bf886d4c-aafd-5159-a9c1-995083aabdef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf886d4c-aafd-5159-a9c1-995083aabdef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472995} :answer-field/id #uuid "18afe69d-87a8-5ebb-b3e6-8e65d1302ff0" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "202c6abf-dfb5-522a-9256-456835ce12bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-214430" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={1,3,5,7}$ are transitive?\n- **I.** ${R}_{1}=∅$\n- **II.** ${R}_{2}={A}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${R}_{3}={(5,5)}$\n- **IV.** ${R}_{4}={(5,1),(7,3)}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050335 :topic/id #uuid "40d51ec6-50e2-56a3-b67d-694483590810" :topic/math-academy-id 4822 :topic/title "Transitive Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a299c676-d4bd-5e7c-9a2c-0554e7b11068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472996} {:answer/id #uuid "94f3ebe8-49c9-50b3-a868-e5d33ab917ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94f3ebe8-49c9-50b3-a868-e5d33ab917ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472997} :answer-field/id #uuid "174d226d-d258-5803-bd1a-f8c8044e8e61" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e37eca59-a651-5ae5-a63a-f7c00a6e9ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472998} {:answer/id #uuid "d58460b0-5da4-55d1-9273-6866444c5ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d58460b0-5da4-55d1-9273-6866444c5ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472999} :answer-field/id #uuid "64720d48-f6ec-5b01-9b7a-d890a7e21109" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473894} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2625fa0-6fd0-5b37-8bcb-85e89aaf9f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473000} {:answer/id #uuid "6549efa0-c3e5-513f-a9c4-a86cc73d7989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6549efa0-c3e5-513f-a9c4-a86cc73d7989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473001} :answer-field/id #uuid "1908c829-a51f-5ba0-bef7-0ed5edbdf979" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead24d32-9ef3-517d-bc6c-fa5541e27250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473002} {:answer/id #uuid "9afd106d-a0c9-585d-8d8b-f5c66a4f5e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9afd106d-a0c9-585d-8d8b-f5c66a4f5e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473003} :answer-field/id #uuid "586bc858-e57d-5502-9bc1-05c0b7811913" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44cd3428-3b1c-5e19-9525-5e86c3ca616a" :question/math-academy-id "q-214431" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={♢,♡,♠,♣}$ are transitive?\n- **I.** ${R}_{1}={(♢,♢),(♣,♣)}$\n- **II.** ${R}_{2}={(♠,♠)}$\n- **III.** ${R}_{3}={(♢,♠),(♡,♣)}$\n- **IV.** ${R}_{4}=∅$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947f058c-4c47-52df-8c28-aed016d2b148" :knowledge-point/title "Transitive Relations Defined On Infinite Sets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050335 :topic/id #uuid "40d51ec6-50e2-56a3-b67d-694483590810" :topic/math-academy-id 4822 :topic/title "Transitive Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4570740-e38d-5619-a596-b27434de2acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472976} {:answer/id #uuid "2445e097-f060-51a2-9b4f-786c2564057b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2445e097-f060-51a2-9b4f-786c2564057b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472977} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2928ca7-454a-561d-a28f-37c83fce06c0" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a72744f6-7c6e-5b96-8a5c-21c64adc05c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472978} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8c1316-7031-5bf4-b390-e30b6262259d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a72744f6-7c6e-5b96-8a5c-21c64adc05c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472978} :answer-field/id #uuid "540b176a-d122-565a-8e22-9c2f73677d72" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0db7216-4608-5567-9227-6f5edb180769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472980} {:answer/id #uuid "808ff085-7391-520c-bb65-0806364a6656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "808ff085-7391-520c-bb65-0806364a6656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472981} :answer-field/id #uuid "7315c86a-eebd-57ce-ac6f-9eaaeeb953b9" :answer-field/key "T" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70eb9024-0964-59ad-a103-f6948d25a78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214002" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are transitive?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **R.** $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- **S.** $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y<4,$ defined on $R$\n\n- **T.** $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,x)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx∣y\\,\\text{and}\\,y∣z\\,⟹\\,x∣z\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,-4)\\in S$ since $1+(-4)=-3<4,$ and\n\n- $(-4,5)\\in S$ since $-4+5=1<4,$ but\n\n- $(1,5)\\notin S$ since $1+5=6≮4.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ and $(y,x)\\in T,$ then $(x,z)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,\\text{and}\\,y≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟹\\,x≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $T$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613 :topic/id #uuid "aeb25c88-ca51-51cd-bde9-b35abbd14a79" :topic/math-academy-id 2102 :topic/title "Orthogonal Complements"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7c78237-7ba8-5f5d-a33b-f0e13b1bcaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454048} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ae988e-f035-54a7-a23f-739a60fc9826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7c78237-7ba8-5f5d-a33b-f0e13b1bcaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454048} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a1a93b-7a1e-598e-9ac9-1fec0a8541af" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1121815-b4cb-5a43-b5a6-e6c5f54f549f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454050} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc194be-1802-5b3c-9ead-0634aaac6f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdc194be-1802-5b3c-9ead-0634aaac6f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454051} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c705186-a967-52c8-8f4b-6be1b77a56ab" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac97a943-76b0-577f-974e-60a75af70b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454052} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3399ef-8ee6-5f98-9847-2e8f4d1a6f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac97a943-76b0-577f-974e-60a75af70b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454052} :answer-field/id #uuid "b18f9b5e-6144-516e-bca1-6e1944599bbe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac83e377-f632-58f6-8bed-4004335968a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80514" :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **II.** $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **III.** $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1=-8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613 :topic/id #uuid "aeb25c88-ca51-51cd-bde9-b35abbd14a79" :topic/math-academy-id 2102 :topic/title "Orthogonal Complements"} :activity/title "Orthogonal Complements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418736} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418773}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150 :topic/id #uuid "deab20e7-248d-57af-ad42-5509f60f2d31" :topic/math-academy-id 3360 :topic/title "Path Independence of Line Integrals"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbc76e1-b401-5a78-abad-35dac56a605e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453342} {:answer/id #uuid "279f518b-43b4-54ff-a27b-f222f36cb0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "279f518b-43b4-54ff-a27b-f222f36cb0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453343} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab559b0-8ad8-5600-af4d-58237670463b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2848ae05-afe6-565a-9e41-aea348ad8825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453344} {:answer/id #uuid "b066d196-4087-59b9-80a0-674602ceaaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b066d196-4087-59b9-80a0-674602ceaaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453345} :answer-field/id #uuid "029fd954-167e-57fe-8991-0827db39c02c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454422} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa9edb55-47b4-5ffa-8f84-e71046dcce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453346} {:answer/id #uuid "9b304306-6e7d-5cf9-b636-c3306a1cc7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa9edb55-47b4-5ffa-8f84-e71046dcce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453346} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb5c926-ae08-5e20-a8c6-c74a02337973" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2382595d-1329-5487-addb-48a96fc302ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453348} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaebe90-5c00-5752-8300-47cb5c96cc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2382595d-1329-5487-addb-48a96fc302ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453348} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ae2286-b965-58b3-9eee-07c2cde691c4" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b47df0de-b5ee-5562-b36a-a659cbe48d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-125796" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction.\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨0,\\,1-2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2t-1,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150 :topic/id #uuid "deab20e7-248d-57af-ad42-5509f60f2d31" :topic/math-academy-id 3360 :topic/title "Path Independence of Line Integrals"} :activity/title "Path Independence of Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549301} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550114}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048934 :topic/id #uuid "cd3b7077-78e5-5eb6-880e-d8648790c6b0" :topic/math-academy-id 2838 :topic/title "Modeling With the Poisson Distribution"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fafa6def-aaf0-5ba2-855c-6a88e1f98666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473476} {:answer/i