wer-field/id #uuid "9019b7d6-83fb-5a8e-9381-2f56267cbd57" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2963275e-8991-5613-bcc4-d15bd0b82d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-85317" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n- **I.** The number of defective televisions manufactured by a company in a month\n- **II.** The grade point average of a college class\n- **III.** The average speed of a car during a trip\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of defective televisions can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The grade point average of a college class is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average speed of a car is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048934 :topic/id #uuid "cd3b7077-78e5-5eb6-880e-d8648790c6b0" :topic/math-academy-id 2838 :topic/title "Modeling With the Poisson Distr