nswer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fab7b2b9-4fb5-56fc-9278-9df96447599b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75553" :question/problem "Which of the following is a variable?\n\nI. $b$\n\nII. $b+c$\n\nIII. $c$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each one in turn.\n\nI. $b$ is a variable because it's a single letter by itself.\n\nII. $b+c$ is an algebraic expression, not a variable.\n\nIII. $c$ is a variable because it's a single letter by itself.\n\nSo, only choices I and III represent a variable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08481a62-77df-5d28-8698-8d4f00950869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474416} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fc6aad-598a-5eea-a01d-073014ef806b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8fc6aad-598a-5eea-a01d-073014ef806b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474417} :answer-field/id #uuid "77ec49cf-320f-50a7-b023-4738f2e9171a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475134} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a349c90-01d0-5ad9-81e5-e6ed9f0c6c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474418} {:answer/id #uuid "8298d6ae-f3e5-5239-a746-af5a429289ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8298d6ae-f3e5-5239-a746-af5a429289ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474419} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d802c17-6ac3-5bd3-bf5d-a4c39b4ce963" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f28e00-3e29-5db8-87ab-aefad47578e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474420} {:answer/id #uuid "e87c29df-7f86-5b0d-8272-11405cd25962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e87c29df-7f86-5b0d-8272-11405cd25962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474421} :answer-field/id #uuid "7790cb62-e714-5a36-87b0-09aa86dc3f4a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475136} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cdd8b16-2df5-5a90-b9b9-15e1e44f9c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474422} {:answer/id #uuid "96ee3aee-80f2-5eca-80b9-adb92cb652ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ee3aee-80f2-5eca-80b9-adb92cb652ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474423} :answer-field/id #uuid "353fb675-d574-5a12-8c87-8a3853c61877" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ea10c69-304c-5ad4-bd04-4affc2cf325b" :question/math-academy-id "q-157376" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(-1,3,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 3t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-a)+(-b) \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(-a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638 :topic/id #uuid "0748b9c2-fae7-5522-9655-db9e6f1d50c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1347 :topic/title "Probability Density Functions of Continuous Random Variables"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd58f32-7f1c-557f-ae42-5120bfbcb2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474966} {:answer/id #uuid "fd62a3d9-0c24-5905-966e-65252ccd83d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fd58f32-7f1c-557f-ae42-5120bfbcb2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474966} :answer-field/id #uuid "436cc2ae-79ed-5267-adeb-2b7068b12807" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475409} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ddde048-6382-55d9-ad53-99efc8944351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474968} {:answer/id #uuid "c616d525-6bf4-56fb-b189-7bf6e8974588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ddde048-6382-55d9-ad53-99efc8944351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474968} :answer-field/id #uuid "935ad41a-97b9-56bc-a04c-a116b96c9924" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475410} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad2933b3-b638-5a31-9303-cf86416c9315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474970} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb03621-3f0d-5fb9-a66b-ee2651bd3643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb03621-3f0d-5fb9-a66b-ee2651bd3643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474971} :answer-field/id #uuid "e41f053d-a3a7-5269-bbe8-0b2ce3377b46" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2365a6d7-b820-5041-9972-25df8cfa8866" :question/math-academy-id "q-86922" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\nI. The outcome of rolling a $10$-sided die\n\nII. The number of aces in a standard deck of playing cards\n\nIII. The weight of a randomly selected person, measured in kilograms\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,10,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The number of aces in a standard deck of playing cards is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $4$ aces in a standard deck of playing cards.)\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/title "Quiz 4" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425855} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425881}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0775b041-2fcc-5161-beb5-80cd60e4af23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474960} {:answer/id #uuid "f695d431-d46b-54df-b510-3aea6bbccfdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0775b041-2fcc-5161-beb5-80cd60e4af23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474960} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b9ce56f-633d-52a6-9fe3-5e31c2ec3104" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475406} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2c39ea-ff23-5ab6-97b2-01c12343ec12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474962} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0dbf92-3cec-57d5-bf09-b54ff477f7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e2c39ea-ff23-5ab6-97b2-01c12343ec12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474962} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfbefaa-3aac-5745-9c77-f042b1776296" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475407} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "787b4942-1ca4-5050-825d-4ac096b82f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474964} {:answer/id #uuid "b98f4c53-ec17-539a-b8eb-fcaada4b3fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b98f4c53-ec17-539a-b8eb-fcaada4b3fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474965} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f93c2fb-74eb-51e3-8d97-e03de7254f93" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1654ded7-c0bc-579c-a9e9-6daa6a1243e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-86894" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d8f57afa16e99c13e6e36c5544a7c7594da13321bd3d01692a5a296a20953d7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/ce/ce9b9f3435a94b3f9f87b195e5b1591c80281b23cfb76ceb8e79cdeec3ae66b0.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and II. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function III equals $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425910} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425934} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481765} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482631}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c96195d-9858-594d-bb72-8ff337d223f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474906} {:answer/id #uuid "ac21b789-f73c-5a27-a558-3259fd8be378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c96195d-9858-594d-bb72-8ff337d223f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474906} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb5c3d2-18c2-5791-91fb-de36d351c3f3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475379} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab7c8fd-5e59-504d-87ca-07f4eaf0b228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474908} {:answer/id #uuid "e456c4ef-ec5d-5bb0-bee8-fd0487478461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e456c4ef-ec5d-5bb0-bee8-fd0487478461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474909} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc86775b-88dd-5f7c-98f3-98d7a6ccc3be" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475380} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b9bb14-06f2-5942-afb1-747bedec4368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474910} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9391cb-f39e-56c2-b392-ee39256bc2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80b9bb14-06f2-5942-afb1-747bedec4368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474910} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a36324a-0315-506f-92d5-cf0da1cac767" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19ad1761-3136-5be8-b038-5aa62265f48b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80526" :question/problem "![](images/88/8866056ce5c7c80ed8452246cc3a40074b6841615ed6ef472e7a1612709e51fa.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{R}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,1]\\times [-3,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425910} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425932}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048592 :topic/id #uuid "ac50c7f3-cf1f-565d-96af-32bd8ce3b7b9" :topic/math-academy-id 4254 :topic/title "Truth Sets of Predicates"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f548d9d7-5c43-5510-85e7-89f665103530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474544} {:answer/id #uuid "299c3818-e21a-5d63-9d43-edb435384b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "299c3818-e21a-5d63-9d43-edb435384b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474545} :answer-field/id #uuid "33029c5d-a58f-5846-b20f-d30fb9ae1431" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475198} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cffbbad6-2ff2-508a-a394-ef1effcca096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474546} {:answer/id #uuid "966aa064-e076-50ec-9fdb-88187a6d9994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cffbbad6-2ff2-508a-a394-ef1effcca096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474546} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eb33668-d751-5e1f-a8f1-d04525bc5ad1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475199} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3099e3c-5a65-5e1b-a4c9-9aceb7935c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474548} {:answer/id #uuid "517d0ea8-bd34-5d06-9d04-f78f5ae6a208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "517d0ea8-bd34-5d06-9d04-f78f5ae6a208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474549} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc37e72-ebc3-54f5-abd0-30ce43dacb22" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86866e7c-73c9-5409-8743-b71ad4d3cbe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-175366" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $P(y):$ ${y}^{2}-1=8$\n\nII. $Q(a,b):$ ${a}^{2}+{b}^{2}$\n\nIII. $R(x,y):$ $\\frac{x}{y}\\in N\\cap Q$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(a,b)$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d5ed8d-7e60-5db5-b7d1-d96bb65185a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474362} {:answer/id #uuid "10b14067-3f7f-5868-8791-43396e0607b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b14067-3f7f-5868-8791-43396e0607b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474363} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb207fd4-1ade-5c72-8528-ba143a738870" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0948d764-f192-5b49-80f0-17ce4b9bcefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474364} {:answer/id #uuid "f2aade65-5d3a-520b-86ea-745a85070e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0948d764-f192-5b49-80f0-17ce4b9bcefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474364} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b6ddd3-8ce7-5c7f-874e-23d5563021dc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475108} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dc218b-818c-594c-978b-8b69736909ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474366} {:answer/id #uuid "4999264b-817a-578e-85f5-209e47509eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4999264b-817a-578e-85f5-209e47509eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474367} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ecf0bb-4395-5d51-99bd-1c0a67d30176" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "251cfe7e-474d-59b2-8ccf-101401daf068" :question/math-academy-id "q-139978" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero\n\nIII. $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $det(A)=0,$ the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero.* $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn,  means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis so the dimension must be greater than zero.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))\\ne 5,$ which means that $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484809} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485601}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2024e7-8603-5e5a-8b2e-7cb9aa759b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453380} {:answer/id #uuid "83ac7d4b-d4c5-5c92-8589-e7ec5bddcb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83ac7d4b-d4c5-5c92-8589-e7ec5bddcb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453381} :answer-field/id #uuid "89392c96-2fc2-5a4d-8885-bb32eafcba1e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454440} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79098b74-c172-54b9-82e2-49824d97c9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453382} {:answer/id #uuid "0ffeb813-fd36-5776-8ba7-23c839dba453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79098b74-c172-54b9-82e2-49824d97c9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453382} :answer-field/id #uuid "be118244-9178-534b-8fc7-3f7c4f68e4fc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05113400-1012-5e94-b50e-c93f67bd0a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453384} {:answer/id #uuid "75b2fb9a-b3d9-55ad-8209-77f57b32b7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b2fb9a-b3d9-55ad-8209-77f57b32b7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453385} :answer-field/id #uuid "994ca1fa-debf-5f93-b9db-34b6b1ce3215" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0f098cb-e297-54fe-a435-e8a16d43aa06" :question/math-academy-id "q-139977" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$\n\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(A)<4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb17f7e3-269e-5625-a111-f4d4af48c417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474272} {:answer/id #uuid "31d11665-b6a6-55f9-a8b1-68377f92906b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb17f7e3-269e-5625-a111-f4d4af48c417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474272} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1b2942f-e716-57f1-88e3-40e92d8e3656" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ad710ac-351e-551c-9aa3-eb471c6ba7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474274} {:answer/id #uuid "ff323ad8-0d05-55f5-9ec9-2dd89adb86b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ad710ac-351e-551c-9aa3-eb471c6ba7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474274} :answer-field/id #uuid "189d1c47-187f-5ca5-b080-65e61257620b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b68cef7-62b5-5d0d-8003-74ec4afeca09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474276} {:answer/id #uuid "e73f1446-6ae4-598e-9a58-e6999a487413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e73f1446-6ae4-598e-9a58-e6999a487413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474277} :answer-field/id #uuid "58329b77-3f5c-5c03-892f-99af51f6bc0b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "784a3447-3ec5-5b3c-aa05-c4909c41863a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110763" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨1+t,t-1⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:xy=0\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=0\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1+t \\\\ t-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}t+1 &=0 \\\\ t-1 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0⟩+⟨0,1⟩=⟨1,1⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xy=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a line (the $y$-axis), and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b1f26e-74e3-54dd-9a19-c2d7237c25a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453526} {:answer/id #uuid "4b07ac45-b4fe-591c-9ec8-4c6f325efb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06b1f26e-74e3-54dd-9a19-c2d7237c25a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453526} :answer-field/id #uuid "02495e60-1290-53ef-9f15-de2782ac8189" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e976187d-1a3c-5254-ac57-00139179499a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453528} {:answer/id #uuid "bca2a77d-be2d-5162-9b1f-b5aabfcce26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e976187d-1a3c-5254-ac57-00139179499a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453528} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2dcd84-25cf-5410-9806-8fbaad74db0c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af1f792c-ea17-539c-b803-70174e0206c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453530} {:answer/id #uuid "00f766d5-13c9-54cf-8c6a-17327b8856ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f766d5-13c9-54cf-8c6a-17327b8856ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453531} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded477a5-7ff9-5d55-972d-d2753c0b4f0c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42098bc3-a0a9-510c-94ee-f777802bb49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162666" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}{a}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a\\in Z.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $A\\in R,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\n- **II.** For any $A\\in R,$ we have $2\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\n- **III.** For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example,the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in R,$ since $4={2}^{2}$ and $2\\in Z.$ However\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin R,\n$$\n\nbecause a negative number ($-4$) can't be a square.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $R.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n2\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\notin R\n$$\n\nbecause the $22$-entry of $2\\cdot A$ is not equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426537} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426554}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa6071c0-fff9-534e-9586-a01bcfff63d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453496} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fbd9b-de38-5c14-bd68-eddf37af75ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fbd9b-de38-5c14-bd68-eddf37af75ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453497} :answer-field/id #uuid "868be7d6-6944-5cfb-b5c7-52473d4ca9b8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183add14-81ee-5c40-a875-870eb4d49183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453498} {:answer/id #uuid "fcea62b3-8084-56ff-8cb4-1d53d269fe8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "183add14-81ee-5c40-a875-870eb4d49183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453498} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2b928b0-206b-5ef1-be69-8e64cd6dfcb3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47cf2934-bf49-5738-8cbd-42bb2111933c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453500} {:answer/id #uuid "fadcb3d1-7cde-5c14-83fa-f208111194de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47cf2934-bf49-5738-8cbd-42bb2111933c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453500} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ded8d53-6371-5429-8b4f-32fe142e8588" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca37d526-e95e-5c72-ad4b-d05af7166afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162394" :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ that satisfy $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The exponential function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\in E$\n\n- **II.** For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in E.$\n\n- **III.** The set $E$ is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\nf(0)={e}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\in E.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the function $f(x)=g(x)={e}^{x}\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n(f+g)(0)={e}^{0}+{e}^{0}=1+1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin E.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $E$ is not closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426537} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426553}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7fcec0d8-ca03-5446-a42f-65d0ccddd584" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Quadrant of a Given Point" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517 :topic/id #uuid "50123c1c-7113-541e-8c4f-047f2eec416d" :topic/math-academy-id 92 :topic/title "The Cartesian Coordinate System"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a8e17a0-daa3-5ce7-9699-c81f32093d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474502} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdf29b7-66c8-593e-b845-6514eb9e9b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a8e17a0-daa3-5ce7-9699-c81f32093d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474502} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fffa765-2a81-5787-b94f-54d3ea9dd2fd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475177} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5ee7af1-568a-504f-a7b5-3dc1b7f3487c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474504} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa59f6c-a796-5468-b924-eb34bdd4593e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ee7af1-568a-504f-a7b5-3dc1b7f3487c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474504} :answer-field/id #uuid "115d02b4-a68c-542d-a036-9f4eff58e7c2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c90982-a31c-5706-b5d8-903cc7b07e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474506} {:answer/id #uuid "db66a6db-2f22-5d97-81b6-da2ae8fa5104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db66a6db-2f22-5d97-81b6-da2ae8fa5104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474507} :answer-field/id #uuid "34975eac-6fea-5e8a-8221-0a3c35d408d7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9298397c-357d-5783-87f8-a9a66297312a" :question/math-academy-id "q-169184" :question/problem "![](images/92/928b00ea1ac59e758c4bc9e224d2d27e1c2149e31d0c5c3ab7673ee9abeae208.png)\n\nWhich of the following is true regarding the point $S?$\n\nI. It is in the fourth quadrant\n\nII. Its $x$-coordinate is $1$\n\nIII. Its $y$-coordinate is $-3$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The point $S$ is in the third quadrant, so statement I is false.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the point $S,$ we start at the origin, move $1$ units *left*, and then $3$ units *down*. \n\n![](images/f1/f150eac73755dc5f39b499b6ca0ba12624e4c27dd79e7207e11f6c88d04b596d.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $S$ are $(-1,-3).$ Hence, statement II is false and statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0526a2-d359-56c1-b909-e8be2980c9c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453398} {:answer/id #uuid "2318b4e8-9946-556e-9713-3affeb4a75e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2318b4e8-9946-556e-9713-3affeb4a75e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453399} :answer-field/id #uuid "60d2051c-cc4a-53b5-bfb1-613e54f12d04" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454449} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc7b6ede-9087-51f0-9391-ac769ed7b7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453400} {:answer/id #uuid "0417573f-1cae-56d6-9bb3-73ee34bed071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0417573f-1cae-56d6-9bb3-73ee34bed071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453401} :answer-field/id #uuid "82709fdc-0e50-5afa-adb3-4e8907d8c7af" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6e4f8b1-a019-545a-9ccc-657e5b06d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453402} {:answer/id #uuid "62da9a85-7410-5922-838a-f4f9375298c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e4f8b1-a019-545a-9ccc-657e5b06d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453402} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c95b926-8fa5-510c-b805-f0ecff86e55f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0be806ce-1a6b-5ee1-964b-e362c3c8599e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140495" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- **II.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- **III.** $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(1)(3)+2(0)(1)+3(2)(-1) \\\\  & =6+0-6 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(-9)+2(0)(-2)+3(2)(3) \\\\  & =-18+0+18 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}.$ Computing the inner product, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(3)(-9)+2(1)(-2)+3(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-54-4-9 \\\\  & =-67\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nSince ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3},$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6f05695-5d5d-5e1b-bfad-77c51f81a863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474374} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc309ff-eb8f-559c-b906-81545a54e0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc309ff-eb8f-559c-b906-81545a54e0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474375} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d94332b-01c8-5d93-bd36-59655b6dc3cf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df48fe1-4c0e-5788-ae44-be72c3a42df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474376} {:answer/id #uuid "674b056b-93cf-5bbe-ae7e-6ce2c2c14c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2df48fe1-4c0e-5788-ae44-be72c3a42df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474376} :answer-field/id #uuid "05746e78-6328-58c4-96d3-034fda58ec6d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475114} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e78717-3155-5738-b52e-432f0cf2cf83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474378} {:answer/id #uuid "f66fe2c1-0155-5688-b8cc-b89a397d5048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f66fe2c1-0155-5688-b8cc-b89a397d5048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474379} :answer-field/id #uuid "453a25c3-916b-52ae-94c1-1cdb5d5d8d36" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f21c5fc-f92d-5b81-95e0-bb89748670fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-140497" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =5(1)(-1)+(1)(7)+2(1)(-1) \\\\  & =-5+7-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =5(-1)(2)+(7)(0)+2(-1)(-5) \\\\  & =-10+0+10 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =5(1)(2)+(1)(0)+2(1)(-5) \\\\  & =10+0-10 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4a33807-0dc5-58b8-9e96-d36c211cd17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474200} {:answer/id #uuid "630190b1-87c4-5c43-bc91-504b4950f58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "630190b1-87c4-5c43-bc91-504b4950f58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474201} :answer-field/id #uuid "4790b87e-09c0-53d0-8040-c5d65661c89f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475026} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2983d130-4a1c-5f7c-92f8-2b8f8ef9258b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474202} {:answer/id #uuid "5a01831f-984b-5618-8beb-6e5a83c9d9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a01831f-984b-5618-8beb-6e5a83c9d9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474203} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ee9bbc-f25e-576b-8ecb-a39f90ca2847" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c407a64-fdc1-57ce-9336-528dd1775a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474204} {:answer/id #uuid "b332a85e-6f82-5133-8e3a-be50909f94ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c407a64-fdc1-57ce-9336-528dd1775a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474204} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c2d9797-b732-51a2-940c-b09caf6ff041" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3a213f9-9ffe-56d7-a8aa-efb68c3978ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-104735" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+1,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(3(-1))\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(3(0))\\cdot (0+1)+(3(1))\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{-1}\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1){|}_{-1}+(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{1}\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(3(-1))\\cdot ((-1)^{2}-1)+(3(0))\\cdot ({0}^{2}-1)+(3(1))\\cdot ({1}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f67cf98-c041-56b4-b675-cd6608fe0e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475020} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa3734e-111b-53f2-8ebe-871dfb1f88e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f67cf98-c041-56b4-b675-cd6608fe0e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475020} :answer-field/id #uuid "17fd2243-0f57-5f80-9382-57761ade6958" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e85a7d43-b6d8-581b-9e5f-5f063c7c4b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475022} {:answer/id #uuid "b53eb6b7-af91-5d0b-8d68-a78f87a43b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b53eb6b7-af91-5d0b-8d68-a78f87a43b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475023} :answer-field/id #uuid "623fd893-1fe0-5fbf-984e-f62b33160f96" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "113ff175-e867-564a-8a13-cc2d64307b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475024} {:answer/id #uuid "72498a25-b060-53eb-8bae-7190cb5973bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72498a25-b060-53eb-8bae-7190cb5973bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475025} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac4eba3-941d-510f-b8db-bb23b5d39486" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1da803c-87de-5156-b3a7-d636001e9616" :question/math-academy-id "q-99637" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(2)(1)+3(-1)(1)+(1)(-1) \\\\  & =4-3-1 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(0)+3(1)(1)+(-1)(3) \\\\  & =0+3-3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(2)(0)+3(-1)(1)+(1)(3) \\\\  & =0-3+3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866 :topic/title "The Dimension of a Span"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4aa840c-676d-579d-907a-45c66e348cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454000} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfd99bd-c5b6-5c79-9329-4d49aaf5514c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dfd99bd-c5b6-5c79-9329-4d49aaf5514c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454001} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1077dc8-5cdf-55a4-9cf4-9f78c96894c1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454750} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b075b0e-71d3-5492-a58e-181a0bd4a531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454002} {:answer/id #uuid "00552208-c5f0-56e1-9eb5-0d86c6ef59df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b075b0e-71d3-5492-a58e-181a0bd4a531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454002} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ff5afd-db04-5235-86fe-8c0c85c46c31" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee0c146-1417-5ad8-bd81-8ddd4be82e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454004} {:answer/id #uuid "80051909-7b26-58b5-b6ce-f8a75cd2c9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80051909-7b26-58b5-b6ce-f8a75cd2c9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454005} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98cc577-1407-5f32-81d7-2d8910452f11" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48a8881c-e6ac-54a2-9375-24bf5efbb3a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79325" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$\n\n- **II.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$\n\n- **III.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So,  ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w\\})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567 :topic/id #uuid "54747772-1cac-572e-9b8f-be3a6c8abe45" :topic/math-academy-id 2198 :topic/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21740e2f-600c-5a93-859b-56d238cd0e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437776} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9be679-baf3-5630-ae88-5b57cb494970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21740e2f-600c-5a93-859b-56d238cd0e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437776} :answer-field/id #uuid "b64b88b3-d28a-5b4a-a38b-7fe8543c12b6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438917} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11bfb2c2-cc66-55c8-bec6-1505afc317a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437778} {:answer/id #uuid "8905b533-fda6-553c-b3a2-e716938166b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8905b533-fda6-553c-b3a2-e716938166b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437779} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfac5d71-e652-5d4b-8ce8-de5480e6fd0d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438918} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5c9797-7316-5214-93dc-7545c27a4470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437780} {:answer/id #uuid "c013a325-7790-52f3-85d6-c6452b7b6dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c013a325-7790-52f3-85d6-c6452b7b6dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437781} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf7a35d-36f8-551d-abfc-0f58ffd44dd7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c0dea55-217c-592d-9a6c-d305c8e67729" :question/math-academy-id "q-126575" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3},\\,{x}^{3}+1⟩$\n\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{2}+\\sqrt{2}x,\\,\\frac{3x}{2}⟩$\n\n- **III.** $F(x,y)=⟨x,\\,x⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{3}{2},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Simple Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050824 :topic/id #uuid "69970c4c-efa5-537d-a31c-79a56da6e61a" :topic/math-academy-id 2524 :topic/title "Simple Harmonic Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ace068e1-ab27-5034-9fdd-889739e4ddd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474972} {:answer/id #uuid "422799e5-23ee-5dc7-b632-76ffec3a5727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ace068e1-ab27-5034-9fdd-889739e4ddd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474972} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8de985-d1e0-59b6-a5c0-3c4549c1a179" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475412} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7496f0c4-2b46-5127-bfa7-c916b74f1f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474974} {:answer/id #uuid "bd89d55e-6590-5655-a89d-bbed83af8e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7496f0c4-2b46-5127-bfa7-c916b74f1f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474974} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7118b37-b721-5850-bd66-c32e1cbb4a4c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55c4ab68-5fcc-5325-b080-fc95a4cc450a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474976} {:answer/id #uuid "14f4fb0e-2d22-512b-ba94-b8520a2b3743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14f4fb0e-2d22-512b-ba94-b8520a2b3743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474977} :answer-field/id #uuid "331f7cdf-6363-5525-b0c4-ce841a0d8619" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3951f87d-bb0f-5303-830e-d70693674542" :question/math-academy-id "q-94279" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)$\n\nTherefore, only statement III could be used to represent  $y(t).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050824 :topic/id #uuid "69970c4c-efa5-537d-a31c-79a56da6e61a" :topic/math-academy-id 2524 :topic/title "Simple Harmonic Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c4657c2-41bb-586d-a78c-c3631c9c62f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453910} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d30694-cffc-52bd-9c84-1a29c603a07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5d30694-cffc-52bd-9c84-1a29c603a07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453911} :answer-field/id #uuid "284e987f-668f-5073-82c4-6d1d6e4c9e59" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454705} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b48675a-5e6c-57c1-a660-43f019d3237f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453912} {:answer/id #uuid "346f0314-00e3-50d1-bbdb-1f7dafaf2152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b48675a-5e6c-57c1-a660-43f019d3237f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453912} :answer-field/id #uuid "f21297d4-1bb6-52a0-9b68-8d0777902ab0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86a669b1-ce96-5301-9b01-0a42a18a7d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453914} {:answer/id #uuid "bae32439-f79c-53cf-9bc6-aa0048497990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86a669b1-ce96-5301-9b01-0a42a18a7d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453914} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d225cd-6ad1-5a38-8acb-2295618fec83" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1d55e9e-973d-5821-a2d2-76fe96bd1d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-64257" :question/problem "A particle of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=30\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+6y=0$\n\n- **II.** The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n\n- **III.** The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{30}{5}=6\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n1\\. $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n2\\. $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n3\\. $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{6}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 3 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613 :topic/id #uuid "aeb25c88-ca51-51cd-bde9-b35abbd14a79" :topic/math-academy-id 2102 :topic/title "Orthogonal Complements"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1585707-1603-5a1b-af59-b5c138a9c074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453392} {:answer/id #uuid "4025897a-73f9-514f-9a24-860879c00094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4025897a-73f9-514f-9a24-860879c00094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453393} :answer-field/id #uuid "9169cb2e-f525-5cde-ad9b-110e6f63dc5a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b57557-ffd1-597f-9af9-6578ee6fa9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453394} {:answer/id #uuid "d94790ed-263f-5f65-b860-b1eed02e2ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80b57557-ffd1-597f-9af9-6578ee6fa9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453394} :answer-field/id #uuid "4529a519-d7e4-51e4-907d-5d12e79418e7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454447} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c7ea78-8c0f-597a-bff9-449b7300f6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453396} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca62772-2c0a-53cd-b01a-f279dc0c6b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c7ea78-8c0f-597a-bff9-449b7300f6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453396} :answer-field/id #uuid "a38bf924-51c4-568e-aabd-bffd18ea78d4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adc7f30b-f5f4-5c71-84b9-9c4c7b9ab1ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-140464" :question/problem "For the vector $u=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\n- **II.** $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\n- **III.** $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-5)=20\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =2\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =2\\cdot 10+1\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0=15\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048574 :topic/id #uuid "aad2999b-dacb-527f-a4a8-1fce51919fe6" :topic/math-academy-id 4257 :topic/title "Conditional Statements With Predicates"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ca7bae-c5f6-5f45-8005-39dc1d336efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474568} {:answer/id #uuid "ef16fdbd-534f-5a56-800d-2826d90b96e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef16fdbd-534f-5a56-800d-2826d90b96e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474569} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc71c1f9-a6aa-5adf-93aa-3ad18db40ff4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475210} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276e0de2-7f03-5fe6-bac6-531d4adac97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474570} {:answer/id #uuid "5d50814e-5f23-562d-83af-541b4e8b00c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "276e0de2-7f03-5fe6-bac6-531d4adac97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474570} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d43b3d-7d54-5b7b-a285-b49ab23b8daa" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2787d35e-8a30-543b-9c6f-b675cf603e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474572} {:answer/id #uuid "c2395884-fbb1-5bc3-873c-dfa0f8ceb3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2395884-fbb1-5bc3-873c-dfa0f8ceb3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474573} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8fda271-0a88-58b9-887c-77bd606c8a3b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0af36684-2b78-59f1-bddf-9a1297846d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-183358" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $x\\in {Z}^{-}.$ \n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\nI. $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}$ if and only if $x\\in {Z}^{-}.$\n\nII. If $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}$ then $x\\in {Z}^{-},$ or if $x\\in {Z}^{-}$ then $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}.$\n\nIII. $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}$ is equivalent to  $x\\in {Z}^{-}.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}$ if and only if $x\\in {Z}^{-}.$\n\n- $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}$ is equivalent to  $x\\in {Z}^{-}.$\n\n- If $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}$ then $x\\in {Z}^{-},$ and if $x\\in {Z}^{-}$ then $x\\in Z∖{N}_{0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130 :topic/id #uuid "2f232786-4262-5d64-9c58-1578e8c629d2" :topic/math-academy-id 2129 :topic/title "QR Factorization"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fafecd-7387-5634-828c-573f1744befa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454214} {:answer/id #uuid "19a442fc-481d-58d8-9541-569ca4faf6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67fafecd-7387-5634-828c-573f1744befa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454214} :answer-field/id #uuid "5edb2284-a520-5008-b822-43d1ea474475" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58652c7b-c953-5c9f-809b-80c39c943d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454216} {:answer/id #uuid "4713a280-f45c-5164-a1e2-ba8421f47c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4713a280-f45c-5164-a1e2-ba8421f47c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454217} :answer-field/id #uuid "6346abcd-d6c5-5edc-add2-dd9c1ca312a0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454858} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63f0bb0f-b477-52fc-b037-337d27feba6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454218} {:answer/id #uuid "2a679340-6bec-5785-8c26-90181ea28dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f0bb0f-b477-52fc-b037-337d27feba6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454218} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded79da7-007a-52a0-869d-714c07ace673" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b19eecb-3ecc-5584-a617-4bf8675e0b01" :question/math-academy-id "q-95917" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q$ is a $4\\times 2$ upper triangular matrix\n\n- **II.** The columns of $Q$ are unit vectors\n\n- **III.** $R$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be a $4\\times 2$ matrix, but is not necessarily upper triangular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are unit vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130 :topic/id #uuid "2f232786-4262-5d64-9c58-1578e8c629d2" :topic/math-academy-id 2129 :topic/title "QR Factorization"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1caac6a-22d0-5cd7-9db0-e0140ddcd909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454220} {:answer/id #uuid "b926ed9f-ad1b-50f1-813b-bcebdb95283d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454221}] :answer-field/correct {:a