or $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{z-1}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}t}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I does *not* match the definition. The integrand $\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{t}^{z-1}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition.  The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral III matches the definition:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}t}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/id #uuid "f4f8286a-494e-5683-85be-d6899a52bb51" :topic/math-academy-id 3289 :topic/title "The Gamma Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8b7241-1245-5d5b-a271-d1237489a963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474212} {:answer/id #uuid "8b991a22-640f-50f4-9d9d-a048962ad7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b991a22-640f-50f4-9d9d-a048962ad7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #