stion/math-academy-id "q-107829" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{1-z}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{1-z}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The integrand ${t}^{z-1}{e}^{t}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/id #uuid "f4f8286a-494e-5683-85be-d6899a52bb51" :topic/math-academy-id 3289 :topic/title "The Gamma Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b2b758f-75d7-5c78-bc71-8078b25bee7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474218} {:answer/id #uuid "21ebef7c-543a-58db-9cc0-9d12cd347cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b2b758f-75d7-5c78-bc71-8078b25bee7b"