db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474935} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb8d1cfa-b24a-51a4-846e-e5c22847e924" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a49592c2-4649-5980-ab80-d5418f7e763f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85422" :question/problem "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $114\\,040\\,32◻$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $0$\n\nII. $5$\n\nIII. $6$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n1+1+4+0+4+0+3+2+◻=15+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $0$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,320,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $15+0=15,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $5$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,325,$ which is not divisible by $2.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $6$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,326,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $15+6=21,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb8fa39c-a982-569a-b2e0-833c51259711" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Number Is Divisible by 3 or 9" :topic/_kn