 integers?\n\nI. $100$\n\nII. $-5$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{9}{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $100$ and $-5$ are integers, but $-\\frac{9}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558 :topic/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers"} :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433582} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3776bd7-7901-5119-8781-5fa760852de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474248} {:answer/id #uuid "34be4cdd-980d-5cf7-afb5-69444aae9cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3776bd7-7901-5119-8781-5fa760852de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474248} :answer-field/id #uuid "6957b9f5-0c7f-5370-ba38-a21eb5e797aa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3467e4a5-c790-515b-90e9-e9524b6b5643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474250} {:answer/id #uuid "b70cee36-191b-582b-b0d3-c85411adf943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3467e4a5-c790-515b-90e9-e9524b6b5643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474250} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d9e9f8-9da1-55b6-8112-a9793448ac85" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95bccbcb-3a47-5abe-9fa0-82e49506d42a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474252} {:answer/id #uuid "270c3557-fa11-5a21-934d-4213ed1483bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "270c3557-fa11-5a21-934d-4213ed1483bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474253} :answer-field/id #uuid "0801da56-074b-503c-9458-9eeace399eb8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72a34fdf-b3e5-500c-96f3-76e3e66d1191" :question/math-academy-id "q-109572" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a59325-3ce6-5d72-900a-a6549e6f35ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453270} {:answer/id #uuid "63399f97-f600-5b4d-9277-9004f25d5b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63399f97-f600-5b4d-9277-9004f25d5b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453271} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb03ed9d-b3dd-5132-bfbe-ccb2df04fee2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e287c0dd-d035-550b-872f-c7e895e3bec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453272} {:answer/id #uuid "1b26befe-fc8e-5235-b353-9ac88f6dbda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e287c0dd-d035-550b-872f-c7e895e3bec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453272} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa43db2a-05ff-5509-a48a-142c9a76be13" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454386} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d868348-18f3-58da-a6ed-45e73067cd1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453274} {:answer/id #uuid "3e88ff03-d431-5fab-aefd-9a60f1450262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d868348-18f3-58da-a6ed-45e73067cd1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453274} :answer-field/id #uuid "78734bd9-b0b4-5000-8278-6ecd43210da2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93f4dbff-c62f-571f-9c49-e5503854925a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109518" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f449b2-97f3-5186-9bd0-39c537f7db13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453258} {:answer/id #uuid "54696726-fb68-57c0-87f4-c1694ab455cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f449b2-97f3-5186-9bd0-39c537f7db13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453258} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd1d85c6-553b-53a4-ae23-7dde028774d2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454379} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e95d9b3-3929-5758-b2da-baceb16ae7ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453260} {:answer/id #uuid "17170d1b-c8fc-5d71-914c-a2fc83b964c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17170d1b-c8fc-5d71-914c-a2fc83b964c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453261} :answer-field/id #uuid "383e95fb-9e75-52f9-b9dc-633a9fc9d7e7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454380} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da26d3c-84b5-5484-b149-e445d8e96bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453262} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6fbae8-8f80-5d45-a5a9-59b416b697f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1da26d3c-84b5-5484-b149-e445d8e96bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453262} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5175e83-6e25-5458-92b9-9cb792d53159" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3d67809-78b0-59dc-9828-ea082eca69bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109476" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41150d82-d7e7-5d1b-9511-2a172cd9b18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453264} {:answer/id #uuid "b718a973-f38a-5035-869e-48e4b419ff1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b718a973-f38a-5035-869e-48e4b419ff1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453265} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae0413b7-8327-5a24-870c-2079b9d12010" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f23e036-9539-557b-a5b3-9d3a82621232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453266} {:answer/id #uuid "41f4de42-fb54-53fa-9a8d-2338942ae501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f23e036-9539-557b-a5b3-9d3a82621232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453266} :answer-field/id #uuid "f297c7e5-5df0-59e8-9f0a-749e59ee56d6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454383} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb3db82-5aae-5e9e-9a87-27b9cef5d582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453268} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae4e359-d368-5e98-b9be-49032667862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bb3db82-5aae-5e9e-9a87-27b9cef5d582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453268} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddde4964-61ba-58e1-b946-d0bdaa59754b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab77c5c3-a1c5-5710-a08d-82e66f9fdecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109488" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fed10178-ef4b-59fa-b96b-8889cbd8b37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453240} {:answer/id #uuid "24f32365-5e6f-5ad2-bcf2-42010d1c1251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fed10178-ef4b-59fa-b96b-8889cbd8b37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453240} :answer-field/id #uuid "b30cc055-118f-56a3-8691-fe26bb97101d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454370} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c57d558e-22a4-52d4-baa1-e5568009d2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453242} {:answer/id #uuid "42c38ee5-6978-557c-8b50-7d9aacae1ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c57d558e-22a4-52d4-baa1-e5568009d2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453242} :answer-field/id #uuid "7254584c-027f-596c-800b-7c741d8a1fb6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454371} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "616ab2e3-1f4e-5ad6-94c8-c9779b3e83cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453244} {:answer/id #uuid "962d94f6-82e6-5823-a373-7d154666009f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "962d94f6-82e6-5823-a373-7d154666009f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453245} :answer-field/id #uuid "07651977-3bff-5ed3-86ec-820cd2110222" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c32d62b6-73f0-5c6b-b445-e1066fb0b96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109242" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+2I$ and $A-2I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector of $A$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147544 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "638e604b-5416-5d3c-a61f-5be645aa255b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements in Context" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049531 :topic/id #uuid "fc9ea30d-c77e-520f-8dab-e177eee4a483" :topic/math-academy-id 2457 :topic/title "Comparing Fractions, Percents, and Decimals"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "825bb7d0-2dd7-5898-b09d-fa36c3af945c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453868} {:answer/id #uuid "b