id #uuid "08559b28-05c3-5429-8dcc-9459d6dd6efb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16a4ed86-6ba4-5b08-b5bc-00c64da3da49" :question/math-academy-id "q-350266" :question/problem "Sana and Talia are students. In the previous semester, the decimal proportion of Sana’s homework assignments that were turned in early was $0.4,$ while $45%$ of Talia’s homework assignments were turned in early. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- **II.** More than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- **III.** Sana had a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5,\n$$\n\nless than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n45%<50%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement III is false. We can compare $0.4$ and $45%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40%\n$$\n\nSince  $40%<45%,$ Sana did not have a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049531 :topic/id #uuid "fc9ea30d-c77e-520f-8dab-e177eee4a483" :topic/math-academy-id 2457 :topic/title "Comparing Fractions, Percents, and Decimals"} :activity/title "Comparing Fractions, Percents, and Decimals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434371} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434393}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353 :topic/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5db4df4b-7f16-5bf3-97ef-12154e024d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474344} {:answer/id #uuid "a810abc8-9133-52e0-b38a-7cd98d485d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a810abc8-9133-52e0-b38a-7cd98d485d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474345} :answer-field/id #uuid "00d91e8a-45b6-5892-bc2a-a67179b8e70e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475098} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd1b4d3-ada7-5034-be47-4a2c6faead74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474346} {:answer/id #uuid "bff721a3-2da9-5289-a2cc-ef93df66a49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfd1b4d3-ada7-5034-be47-4a2c6faead74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474346} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc81e4ee-db86-5159-990a-694c8671c85b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475099} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e70de085-b4c6-51cb-a8d4-dedc837167ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474348} {:answer/id #uuid "84c0aa2f-082c-5e13-9936-93339186e9a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e70de085-b4c6-51cb-a8d4-dedc837167ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474348} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a8464a-7e39-5edc-8ff3-7087cf3b0eb4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3deca36d-f2e0-579a-9131-b3cacf95a814" :question/math-academy-id "q-132448" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time until a laser fails, in hours, in a specific type of machine\n\nII. The number obtained when launching a fair roulette wheel\n\nIII. The number of children in a daycare center\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Laser failure in a particular machine can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the time it takes for the laser to fail.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space. Moreover, the number obtained when launching a roulette wheel is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of children is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353 :topic/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22692ad8-d851-5bb0-8996-acf141436233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474990} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c81568-173f-5be6-8d87-e7c6741f1504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22692ad8-d851-5bb0-8996-acf141436233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474990} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c8ac4b-cf87-59f3-88e3-27f8c4543488" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "322c02b0-2135-52ce-95fb-6fd4c1402967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474992} {:answer/id #uuid "a805a09c-d518-5b6c-85d1-aecdeba71b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a805a09c-d518-5b6c-85d1-aecdeba71b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474993} :answer-field/id #uuid "37131273-895e-550d-98e5-becccee2d3bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475422} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8dd3dac-a9dd-510b-b4af-63687c7b3700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474994} {:answer/id #uuid "022776e4-143b-5b7d-a86d-3405dca99e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8dd3dac-a9dd-510b-b4af-63687c7b3700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474994} :answer-field/id #uuid "a135b251-9cf2-5560-8d66-008a7b4aed7d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a0569d9-4bfc-5b49-a516-f990725fdc02" :question/math-academy-id "q-96681" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time it takes for a taxi to reach any address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nII. The time it takes for a taxi to reach a particular address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nIII. The number of defective electronic components produced in a factory every week\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow an exponential distribution. The times needed to reach different destinations will likely have different average rates.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Journeys along a particular route can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time needed to reach the destination.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective components is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353 :topic/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92b319d9-e620-5f20-a2e1-2102bed366ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474350} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2637be-7aaa-598a-b8d7-03ab479ba031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474351}] :answe