db/ref 17592186475103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8480fc0-38a5-50e6-ac81-ff79f47d27a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132463" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time between customer calls, in minutes, for a particular business\n\nII. The number of winning lottery tickets in a set of $10$ tickets\n\nIII. The number obtained when picking an integer number between $0$ and $15$ at random\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled using an exponential distribution. Calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next call occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of winning lottery tickets is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number obtained is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049367 :topic/id #uuid "ef34739b-f46e-5073-bcb9-1acc5022fae9" :topic/math-academy-id 3864 :topic/title "Sampling Distributions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70b9be53-9395-5371-8ad7-e19949adab26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474398} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6a58fa-bdda-5f09-9d94-437b257bb9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70b9be53-9395-5371-8ad7-e19949adab26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474398} :answer-field/id #uuid "c83dbd2a-d3c6-528a-8958-f6e5c019702d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475125} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3824ed15-0a45-5def-b485-9e17437c3843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474400} {:answer/id #uuid "32980db4-466c-5aff-abb2-12ee818d5f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32980db4-466c-5aff-abb2-12ee818d5f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474401} :answer-field/id #uuid "599d78d3-13a7-5faa-b23f-82c826f49593" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ef7010-3c1d-51c9-886d-e031be64e3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474402} {:answer/id #uuid "badcd2b2-3071-557b-b92e-a8403da30f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ef7010-3c1d-51c9-886d-e031be64e3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474402} :answer-field/id #uuid "482caa84-5ca9-5d2c-85ff-33b19e0b8321" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "673ddc27-8133-50ba-9995-aed461126acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-143551" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is unknown and $\\sigma $ is known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{11},\\mu )$\n\nII. $min({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{10},\\sigma )$\n\nIII. $\\frac{{X}_{1}+{X}_{11}}{{\\mu }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :kn