 a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049367 :topic/id #uuid "ef34739b-f46e-5073-bcb9-1acc5022fae9" :topic/math-academy-id 3864 :topic/title "Sampling Distributions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8e32782-1831-5aab-af8a-338714b954af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474404} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9df090-354a-552f-aa9c-8ccf3d4cc95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c9df090-354a-552f-aa9c-8ccf3d4cc95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474405} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb74cd6-af7e-50c1-bbcc-5902a35c6eb1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475128} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7add6c35-3388-5b93-a11e-0ca47a006576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474406} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ea9f17-3415-540e-892f-1f1ffa093474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4ea9f17-3415-540e-892f-1f1ffa093474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474407} :answer-field/id #uuid "4582b0d6-4e91-5374-9b08-25026344d851" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475129} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa455dad-16d9-5281-a563-4e2f034ffd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474408} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a6fb32-b0b9-5316-93af-b649c89b3f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8a6fb32-b0b9-5316-93af-b649c89b3f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474409} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef9bb50-293b-55b0-a586-543b9d174439" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd7fa324-de44-5c60-a60e-ad08ed2ad90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143554" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both  known, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}|{X}_{i}-\\mu |$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}({X}_{i}+\\sigma )$\n\nIII. $min({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12})$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population mean $\\mu $ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only.\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42472aa9-7d67-5ed4-b98a-d5c155583284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474810} {:answer/id #uuid "50eed6d6-c97a-50b5-8b9a-6beba0cf0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50eed6d6-c97a-50b5-8b9a-6beba0cf0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474811} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca653d76-a75a-51a4-80dd-bb7659f5015c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd6c4a2-af1f-5698-a7b3-9ffe19512167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474812} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9541a5-f46e-5e6b-afb1-b87a0a138a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac9541a5-f46e-5e6b-afb1-b87a0a138a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474813} :answer-field/id #uuid "4675387e-3ac9-5537-9ed6-f89ff87766bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f901af94-c98d-501a-b234-ab17178d0c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474814} {:answer/id #uuid "336c0586-f08c-5ea5-a8bb-a690031bc9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f901af94-c98d-501a-b234-ab17178d0c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474814} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe925f85-a920-529c-848e-b9bb308c481f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f1ed82d-67ec-55b7-826d-30733bc2c88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35575" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=2\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=3x$ since $0=3\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=2$ since $0\\ne 2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"} :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434588} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434596}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7112c714-4962-5b16-8f8d-765d0369b202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474460} {:answer/id #uuid "7adf6cf4-eabd-52b6-b653-73e0cbfd7368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7adf6cf4-eabd-52b6-b653-73e0cbfd7368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474461} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd78636a-3835-5942-a3fd-7e0ee14f45cb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6df957d-bb38-5c53-b163-e6870f5cb3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474462} {:answer/id #uuid "91041c6e-f542-5d01-8196-02c9d9d65543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6df957d-bb38-5c53-b163-e6870f5cb3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474462} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8eed09-27d9-5e21-8524-a1932ae87109" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475157} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb30a766-7900-5d50-86d5-89016ad059f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474464} {:answer/id #uuid "02928f92-aad5-5cb3-9006-996e38c094f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02928f92-aad5-5cb3-9006-996e38c094f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474465} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d43ab0-6331-5687-9312-3d01a7f01c93" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ec0f059-837e-5e3d-9bb4-8d292f2af4e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163781" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=8$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32262a1a-bff1-5e94-bcd1-5c7a732a6c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474466} {:answer/id #uuid "27511163-3eb0-5da9-82e4-484d6fa22922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27511163-3eb0-5da9-82e4-484d6fa22922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474467} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb89c92-951f-50df-b288-923f96cabf37" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d4031d-2906-5c4f-9490-f1fa02c297d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474468} {:answer/id #uuid "f7192339-db35-51bd-a918-939ffb27ef8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45d4031d-2906-5c4f-9490-f1fa02c297d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474468} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca732c93-4b1e-5d49-a724-0ecdb3340ab3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bb810d3-ff1a-5452-b8a4-17bfa2b2c0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474470} {:answer/id #uuid "a060bed7-c467-5f14-8795-41afe76eb093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bb810d3-ff1a-5452-b8a4-17bfa2b2c0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474470} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14f34e2-c714-5326-9b9d-13f4ad52f6ef" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65b2f5b4-89e0-551f-a08f-1b697ac55d76" :question/math-academy-id "q-163786" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=3$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a saddle point of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(2)-1(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7ce264d-034d-56ae-8c45-60644fea8382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474454} {:answer/id #uuid "b69543a5-9507-55c8-9d7b-79018308daff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b69543a5-9507-55c8-9d7b-79018308daff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474455} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8218bbf-3c1e-52b2-9907-9353e67b32bb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475153} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75d60915-0c29-5e83-8567-179f7fad4c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474456} {:answer/id #uuid "580aa846-205c-5379-bee6-c4a4934e8662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "580aa846-205c-5379-bee6-c4a4934e8662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474457} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae56fee8-14af-5459-8bea-5957e04be6b3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ccbe49-da48-5c61-b5d3-2e63af77af3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474458} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2b6be1-ff0a-509a-b0c6-aadaf80dbadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70ccbe49-da48-5c61-b5d3-2e63af77af3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474458} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b57188-14f6-5dd5-b8c7-016e3700f833" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6771fc8-6834-5ff6-940e-46f1464b61f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163778" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)=4$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=1$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cde007a9-b0f6-57f6-b8c8-569dc617a887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474472} {:answer/id #uuid "14c4e553-7773-5d04-9993-af7dbd1b9557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cde007a9-b0f6-57f6-b8c8-569dc617a887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474472} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1bb8b62-465b-5b82-ba1a-9d8aa323116d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9de572aa-0428-548c-97f5-19930fcda97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474474} {:answer/id #uuid "ce46449c-5f4c-5219-ada2-0e7076d87668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9de572aa-0428-548c-97f5-19930fcda97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474474} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b0b738-bbb1-59b2-9af2-7625e85552c1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475163} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c962c53-221d-56e5-a7ba-4a50b74f26e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474476} {:answer/id #uuid "10fdc2c8-4482-572b-a145-9384b2579ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c962c53-221d-56e5-a7ba-4a50b74f26e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474476} :answer-field/id #uuid "28245267-c604-5f8b-af0e-e69a56ca6fbc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5518b1c-8ee2-55b4-900e-3e5d5d3b8c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163788" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=-15$\n\nII. $A$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(3)-4(4)+2(-6) \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-19<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-31<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944 :topic/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b99dcd31-0696-5ae3-a002-0700a699c3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453886} {:answer/id #uuid "b13cab50-ce9f-50ad-b0dc-b0f33b90172e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b13cab50-ce9f-50ad-b0dc-b0f33b90172e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453887} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4b6b9c-5d2a-594c-960e-1c4e75c64792" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df70a2f-70bb-5fcc-ae8d-49c71465bbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453888} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb49d3c-662c-5e17-871f-5d0e954249ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb49d3c-662c-5e17-871f-5d0e954249ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453889} :answer-field/id #uuid "abbc8d9a-f48f-5dd7-ab15-6b078f09b474" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f80279-e5d1-50e6-9fc4-c16efd13157c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453890} {:answer/id #uuid "b82e04f0-14eb-531c-b7af-055ded9e9fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f80279-e5d1-50e6-9fc4-c16efd13157c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453890} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdec8465-2901-52b6-a16f-9134ee75d3b0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b007310c-a11f-531c-b695-1febe8ef0462" :question/math-academy-id "q-37589" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}12{x}^{2}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **III.** The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,0)=det({f}^{″}(-1,0))=8\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)=({f}^{″}(-1,0))_{11}=8>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(-1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0,$ the point $(-1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755 :topic/id #uuid "1125135d-4341-5e5d-9065-381cc0460203" :topic/math-academy-id 942 :topic/title "Closure Properties of Polynomials"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd89b7d2-6956-5664-aa7c-c5efed2f3a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474386} {:answer/id #uuid "82d53a62-4eee-5fd9-943e-d8d7ff8a5186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82d53a62-4eee-5fd9-943e-d8d7ff8a5186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474387} :answer-field/id #uuid "7300281a-6d96-5dba-b857-3d9edc627b87" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5c62eb7-7fec-5430-8f31-d1635f49502d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474388} {:answer/id #uuid "a158c72d-bc91-52ec-8da4-a2e3efecffa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a158c72d-bc91-52ec-8da4-a2e3efecffa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474389} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c7af6d-7bec-5ac7-80b8-d4165dacfc25" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475120} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9817733e-cbc4-50e6-8cac-c1d08b0c157d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474390} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3e937f-ba8a-5a0a-820d-854b0e7c40cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9817733e-cbc4-50e6-8cac-c1d08b0c157d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474390} :answer-field/id #uuid "d02dd56f-9008-5144-8be4-17b8212d0e36" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17b4b8a5-2a1a-5b8f-9d66-afed9881e7a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-141235" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$\n\nII. $(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$\n\nIII. $(1-3{x}^{3})÷(3x)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3{x}^{3}}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3x}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]
